## Install

1. Drop the `Scripts/` folder into your project's `Assets/`.
2. Add the `{{ s.name }}` component to the object (it needs a `Collider`{% if s.detection == "trigger" %}, set **Is Trigger** on){% else %}).{% endif %}
{% if s.detection == "trigger" %}
3. Tag your player `{{ s.player_tag }}` so range detection works.
{% else %}
3. Raycast mode uses `Camera.main` — make sure your player camera is tagged `MainCamera`.
{% endif %}
4. Wire the prompt to your HUD (e.g. the `interaction_prompt` HUD micro):

```csharp
var it = GetComponent<{{ s.name }}>();
it.OnPrompt    += text => hud.Show(text);   // "" hides it
it.OnInteract  += ()   => Debug.Log("Used {{ s.name }}");
{% if variant in ("lever", "button_switch", "door") %}it.OnToggled   += on => Debug.Log("State: " + on);
{% endif %}{% if s.locked %}it.OnLocked    += ()   => Debug.Log("{{ s.name }} is locked");
{% endif %}```
{% if s.locked %}
In a `--compose` build, an Inventory item with effect `unlock` and
`target_id` `{{ s.lock_id }}` fires `{{ s.name }}.UnlockAny("{{ s.lock_id }}")`
automatically (wired in GameContext.cs). Standalone, call
`{{ s.name }}.UnlockAny("{{ s.lock_id }}")` yourself.
{% endif %}

## Behaviour

{% if variant == "pickup" %}
Player enters range → prompt shows → presses `{{ s.interact_key }}` → object deactivates and `OnInteract` fires (hook your inventory add here).
{% elif variant == "door" %}
Toggles open/closed (rotates 90°) each interaction; `OnToggled(bool)` reports the state.
{% elif variant in ("lever", "button_switch") %}
Toggles an on/off state each interaction; `OnToggled(bool)` drives whatever it controls.
{% elif variant == "npc_talk" %}
Fires `OnInteract` so you can open a conversation (pairs with the `dialogue` micro).
{% else %}
Fires `OnInteract` so you can show readable text / a note.
{% endif %}
`Interact()` is public, so a button or cutscene can trigger it directly.
